WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.118
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.418 --> 00:00:08.897
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.197 --> 00:00:17.446
Unit forty-six: Privacy and Surveillance States. In this unit, you will debate surveillance, security and privacy.

4
00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:20.368
mass surveillance.

5
00:00:21.068 --> 00:00:26.679
mass surveillance means monitoring large populations rather than specific suspects.

6
00:00:26.979 --> 00:00:30.673
Mass surveillance raises serious privacy concerns.

7
00:00:31.373 --> 00:00:34.838
Your turn. Say: mass surveillance.

8
00:00:37.538 --> 00:00:38.992
mass surveillance.

9
00:00:39.983 --> 00:00:41.762
surveillance capitalism.

10
00:00:42.462 --> 00:00:48.026
surveillance capitalism means an economy where companies profit from collecting personal data.

11
00:00:48.326 --> 00:00:51.742
Surveillance capitalism turns our behaviour into profit.

12
00:00:52.442 --> 00:00:56.023
Your turn. Say: surveillance capitalism.

13
00:00:58.723 --> 00:01:00.386
surveillance capitalism.

14
00:01:01.383 --> 00:01:02.721
state monitoring.

15
00:01:03.421 --> 00:01:07.986
state monitoring means government observation of citizens' activities.

16
00:01:08.286 --> 00:01:11.563
Some citizens accept state monitoring for security.

17
00:01:12.263 --> 00:01:15.508
Your turn. Say: state monitoring.

18
00:01:18.208 --> 00:01:19.487
state monitoring.

19
00:01:20.483 --> 00:01:21.914
biometric data.

20
00:01:22.614 --> 00:01:27.690
biometric data means physical information such as fingerprints or face scans.

21
00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:30.965
Airports increasingly collect biometric data.

22
00:01:31.665 --> 00:01:34.735
Your turn. Say: biometric data.

23
00:01:37.435 --> 00:01:38.785
biometric data.

24
00:01:39.783 --> 00:01:41.144
privacy rights.

25
00:01:41.844 --> 00:01:46.445
privacy rights means legal protections for personal information and private life.

26
00:01:46.745 --> 00:01:49.847
Privacy rights must keep pace with technology.

27
00:01:50.547 --> 00:01:53.722
Your turn. Say: privacy rights.

28
00:01:56.422 --> 00:01:57.655
privacy rights.

29
00:01:58.650 --> 00:01:59.802
chilling effect.

30
00:02:00.502 --> 00:02:06.194
chilling effect means when people avoid legal actions because they fear being watched.

31
00:02:06.494 --> 00:02:09.863
Surveillance can create a chilling effect on free speech.

32
00:02:10.563 --> 00:02:13.506
Your turn. Say: chilling effect.

33
00:02:16.206 --> 00:02:17.288
chilling effect.

34
00:02:18.283 --> 00:02:19.598
data retention.

35
00:02:20.298 --> 00:02:24.886
data retention means keeping records of communications for a period of time.

36
00:02:25.186 --> 00:02:28.719
Data retention laws require companies to store records.

37
00:02:29.419 --> 00:02:32.860
Your turn. Say: data retention.

38
00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:36.770
data retention.

39
00:02:37.783 --> 00:02:39.121
social credit.

40
00:02:39.821 --> 00:02:44.433
social credit means a system that scores citizens' or companies' behaviour.

41
00:02:44.733 --> 00:02:47.975
Critics worry that social credit systems limit freedom.

42
00:02:48.675 --> 00:02:52.186
Your turn. Say: social credit.

43
00:02:54.886 --> 00:02:56.178
social credit.

44
00:02:57.183 --> 00:02:59.438
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:59.738 --> 00:03:04.524
Ms. Sarah, cameras are being installed on every street corner in many cities.

46
00:03:05.817 --> 00:03:10.280
Yes. They're said to reduce crime, but the evidence is mixed.

47
00:03:11.583 --> 00:03:15.046
And facial scans are now required at some airports.

48
00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:20.927
Biometric data is being collected on an unprecedented scale.

49
00:03:22.217 --> 00:03:23.438
Is there a risk?

50
00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:31.486
Researchers warn of a chilling effect: people may stop expressing opinions if they feel watched.

51
00:03:32.783 --> 00:03:36.048
Companies collect data too, not only governments.

52
00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:42.533
That's surveillance capitalism. Privacy rights need to be strengthened against both.

53
00:03:43.817 --> 00:03:47.105
Grammar focus: Advanced passives (mastery).

54
00:03:47.405 --> 00:03:51.041
Mastering passives helps you discuss policy objectively.

55
00:03:51.341 --> 00:03:55.457
Cameras are being installed everywhere. They are said to reduce crime.

56
00:03:55.757 --> 00:03:58.012
Privacy rights need to be strengthened.

57
00:03:58.312 --> 00:04:05.153
Mastery of passives lets you discuss policy objectively, without naming responsible actors unless necessary.

58
00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:08.976
Now read and listen. Watched All the Time?.

59
00:04:09.276 --> 00:04:13.749
In many cities, citizens are now recorded hundreds of times a day.

60
00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:20.788
Cameras, phones and online platforms all collect information about our movements and habits.

61
00:04:22.083 --> 00:04:26.567
Supporters argue that state monitoring helps prevent crime and terrorism.

62
00:04:27.883 --> 00:04:32.611
Critics warn that mass surveillance can be misused against ordinary citizens.

63
00:04:33.917 --> 00:04:38.737
Biometric data, once collected, cannot be changed like a password.

64
00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:46.821
Meanwhile, private companies profit from personal data in what has been called surveillance capitalism.

65
00:04:48.117 --> 00:04:53.483
Studies suggest that people who feel watched are less likely to express controversial opinions.

66
00:04:54.783 --> 00:05:00.463
Balancing security and privacy rights may be one of the defining challenges of this century.

67
00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:04.437
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:04.737 --> 00:05:09.384
When people avoid legal actions because they feel watched, it is a blank.

69
00:05:09.684 --> 00:05:15.473
A, chilling effect. B, social credit. C, data retention.

70
00:05:19.483 --> 00:05:22.272
The answer is A: chilling effect.

71
00:05:23.783 --> 00:05:25.771
New cameras blank at the moment.

72
00:05:26.071 --> 00:05:31.304
A, are installing. B, are being installed. C, have installing.

73
00:05:35.317 --> 00:05:37.746
The answer is B: are being installed.

74
00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:41.157
The system is said blank crime.

75
00:05:41.457 --> 00:05:45.644
A, reducing. B, to reduce. C, reduced.

76
00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:51.975
The answer is B: to reduce.

77
00:05:53.483 --> 00:05:56.365
Physical information like fingerprints is blank.

78
00:05:56.665 --> 00:06:02.153
A, biometric data. B, privacy rights. C, mass surveillance.

79
00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:09.194
The answer is A: biometric data.

80
00:06:10.683 --> 00:06:14.378
According to the text, why is biometric data special?

81
00:06:14.678 --> 00:06:21.292
A, it is cheap. B, it cannot be changed like a password. C, it is public.

82
00:06:25.283 --> 00:06:28.966
The answer is B: it cannot be changed like a password.

83
00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:37.038
Now it's your turn. Part one: Is it acceptable to give up some privacy for greater security?

84
00:06:42.338 --> 00:06:49.098
Part two: Use passive forms: state your position, two arguments and one safeguard.

85
00:06:55.398 --> 00:07:07.213
Some privacy can be given up, but only with limits. Data should be collected for specific purposes, and it must be deleted after a set time. Independent oversight needs to be guaranteed by law.

86
00:07:07.513 --> 00:07:10.534
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:16.397
Great work today! You learned eight new words and advanced passives (mastery).

88
00:07:16.697 --> 00:07:21.984
Today's value is online safety. Keep your password secret and be kind online.

89
00:07:22.284 --> 00:07:25.320
See you in Unit forty-seven. Goodbye!

90
00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:28.644
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

